## 3. Methodology & Execution

1. **Initial Assessment:** - The PCs were not configured with IPs/Subnet Masks/Default Gateways. Neither were
the Switch or the Router. I determined everything would need to be configured from scratch. We set to work on the Router
and Switch first, then the PCs

2. **Configuration / Execution:** - I used the IP Configuration program on the PCs to set up IPv4 Addresses with 
appropriate Subnet Masks and Default Gateways to match our Switch. Then I configured the appropriate settings
on the intermediary devices and checked to ensure everything was properly addresses and configured.

3. **Validation:** - I checked that the PCs could ping between each other and had connectivity to the Switch. After
this I made sure they were able to get to the Router and access the internet as well. All pings were successful. I
determined that the network was functional and working as intended.

## 4. Challenges & Troubleshooting - There was some mild difficulty remembering some of the IP config commands
for the Router and Switch, but my Command Reference Guide came in handy and I was able to complete the task.


* **Issue Encountered:** - PC-A could not ping the default gateway
* **Troubleshooting Steps:** - Went back to doublecheck that the PC had the same Default Gateway as Switch. It did not so I updated it
* **Resolution:** Discovered the port was assigned to the wrong VLAN. Reassigned to VLAN 10, which restored connectivity.]

## 5. Conclusion & Lessons Learned
This Lab taught me a ton about the basic functions of a network and how PCs interact with each other on a LAN.
It is very important to understand how networks work so we can better build & protect them.

**Final Outcome:** - The network was functioning as it should and all PCs and intermediary devices had connectivity.
